# HeroTrak Project Rules & Guidelines

## 1. Project Philosophy & Design Goals
- **Domain:** Hero System 6e Initiative & Turn Tracker.
- **Architecture:** Clean, layered architecture (`lib/core/`, `lib/domain/`, `lib/presentation/`).
- **Target Devices:** Large screens (Desktop & Tablets across macOS, Windows, Linux, iOS, Android).
- **Execution Mode:** No-fluff, test-driven development (TDD).

## 2. Code Quality & Standards
- Keep business logic strictly out of UI widgets. Use Riverpod (or explicit controller providers) for state.
- Ensure all domain models are immutable (`Equatable` or `copyWith` patterns).
- Do not invent non-standard 6e rules. Rely on standard 6e Speed Chart and initiative mechanics unless explicitly configured as a custom action mutation.
- Code must be cleanly formatted and pass `flutter analyze` with zero errors or severe warnings.

## 3. Autonomous TDD Loop Directive
- After creating or modifying features in `lib/`, immediately update or create corresponding unit/widget tests in `test/`.
- Always execute `flutter test` via terminal after completing a task.
- If any test fails, read the error stack trace, diagnose the issue, refactor the code/test, and re-run until 100% of tests pass.

## 4. UI & Interaction Patterns
- Keyboard-first desktop experience: hotkeys must be preserved and supported in presentation layers.
- Non-modal UX preferred: favor inline updates, hotkey triggers, and persistent dock views over popup dialogs.